course: dictionary
lesson: 11181
title: One word, many meanings: wire
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A word can change its meaning from one sentence to the next. Wire can be a thing (a noun) or an action (a verb), and it has special meanings in speech. The words around it are the context, and they tell you which meaning fits.
explain: «The buzz is short and the crash is real: a racing heart, shaky hands, a wired brain at midnight.» Here wired is not about metal at all. The racing heart and shaky hands show that wired means very excited and wide awake.
explain: «A landline phone sends the call along a wire or fibre to an exchange.» Phone, call and sends are your clues. A wire here is a cable that carries signals. Try your meaning in the sentence: "along a metal cable" still makes sense, so you have found the right meaning.
explain: «You should pull a plug out by holding the plug itself, not by yanking the cord, because yanking can damage the wires.» The cord has a plastic cover, and the wires are hidden inside it. Damage is the clue: the wires are the parts that can break.
fact: A wire is a long thin piece of metal that can bend.
fact: An electric wire carries electricity, often inside a plastic cover that helps keep the electricity where it belongs.
fact: To wire something means to put wires in it so it can use electricity.
fact: «Wired» in speech can mean very excited and awake, as in «a wired brain at midnight».
fact: Phrases with wire include «barbed wire» (wire with sharp points, used in fences) and «down to the wire» (until the very end).
exercise: «You should pull a plug out by holding the plug itself, not by yanking the cord.» Find and copy one word that means pulling roughly. | *yanking | plug | holding | itself
exercise: Sam says a wired brain means he has a cable in his head. What does wired mean in speech? | Full of electric wires | Tangled up like a ball of string | Sleepy and slow | *Very excited and wide awake
exercise: «A landline phone sends the call along a wire or fibre to an exchange.» What is a wire in this sentence? | A bird that sings in the morning | *A metal cable that carries signals to another phone | A wooden board for a shelf | A small fish
test: Why does the plastic cover on an electric wire matter? | It helps the wire bend more | It makes the wire shine | *It helps keep the electricity safe inside the wire | It makes the wire heavier
test: Which phrase means until the very end? | Electric wire | Copper wire | Barbed wire | *Down to the wire
exam: «Alexander Graham Bell made a working telephone in 1876, and telegraph messages were sent along wires.» In this sentence, what does wires most likely mean? | Birds sitting on a branch | Strips of wood for building | Cakes with thin icing | *Long thin metal cables that carry electric signals
